Sport asks the upper limb for things normal life does not: throwing, falling, pushing, holding. The injuries are different, and so must be the recovery.

I follow athletes of every level, from the amateur who wants to get back on the bike to the person who competes. The method is the same: understand the movement, repair what is needed, build the return.

# Shoulder instability

The typical injury of contact sports and sports with falls (rugby, football, skiing, motorcycling). In young people the risk of recurrence after the first episode is very high. Arthroscopic stabilisation — Bankart, with remplissage when there is a Hill-Sachs lesion — has a low recurrence rate when well indicated and followed by serious rehabilitation.

# Overuse injuries

In overhead sports (volleyball, tennis, swimming, throwing) the shoulder wears through repetition: cuff tendinopathy, superior labral tears, internal impingement. Here surgery is the exception. The work is on technique, load and kinetic chain, with the physiotherapist and the coach.

# Fractures

Clavicle, humerus, wrist, scaphoid. In athletes the choice between a cast and surgery also considers the calendar: sometimes fixing a fracture shortens recovery and allows safe training to resume earlier.

# Return to sport

I do not give a date: I give three criteria. Full, symmetric range of motion. Recovered, measured strength. Sport-specific movement performed without pain and without apprehension. When all three are there, you go back. The programme is written and shared with your coach.
